Dear Superintendent Muldoon,

With the Federal Highway Administration’s (FHWA) $350 million Wildlife Crossings Pilot Program (23 U.S.C. § 171) providing unprecedented funding for wildlife overpasses, underpasses, and exclusion fencing, National Park Service units have a historic opportunity to eliminate collision hotspots along park boundary highways like CA-120 & CA-140 through Yosemite National Park.

However, assembling competitive federal grant proposals with validated Benefit-Cost Ratios (BCRs) and engineering justification often requires substantial time and outside consulting budgets.

To support your park's capital planning, Corridor (https://corridorwildlife.org) has built an automated mitigation prioritization engine and grant brief generator, provided completely free of charge under the Apache-2.0 open-source license:

1. Statutory FHWA Scoring Alignment: Corridor evaluates Yosemite National Park's highway segments against statutory criteria under 23 U.S.C. § 171—measuring severe crash risk, ecological connectivity for black bear, Sierra bighorn sheep, mule deer, and economic return.

2. Rigorous Economic & Engineering Modeling: The platform applies peer-reviewed mitigation efficacy (86% crash reduction for crossing structures paired with exclusion fencing; Rytwinski et al. 2016) against USDOT comprehensive collision valuations ($14,000 per deer, $45,000 per elk, $83,000 per moose) to determine empirical benefit-cost ratios across custom budget limits.

3. 1-Click 2-Page Grant PDF Generator: Corridor instantly generates publication-grade, two-page FHWA WCPP Project Briefs (/v1/mitigation/{id}/brief.pdf) containing project boundary maps, collision history histograms, ecological benefit matrices, and engineering cost breakdowns—ready for inclusion in grant submissions or joint planning meetings with Caltrans.

Corridor is already in use to model priority highway segments across western national parks. We would be honored to provide your executive and planning staff with a tailored demonstration of the FHWA grant generation tool for Yosemite National Park.

You can inspect the platform and methodology at:
- National Park Service Ranger Hub: https://corridorwildlife.org/rangers/
- Peer-Reviewed Scientific Methodology: https://corridorwildlife.org/methodology/

Thank you for your continuing leadership in protecting the natural heritage of Yosemite National Park.

Warm regards,

